---
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro"
import { people, periods, sources } from "../../lib/data/load.ts"
import { formatSpan } from "../../lib/format.ts"
import { url } from "../../lib/url.ts"

const drafts = people.filter((p) => p.status === "draft").length
const repo = "https://github.com/jamers99/biblia-chronos"
---

<Page title="About" description="What Biblia Chronos is, where its data comes from, and how its dates work.">
  <h1>About Biblia Chronos</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Biblia Chronos helps people explore the Bible through timelines, maps, and genealogies: how the people, places,
      and events of Scripture fit together. It's open source, and everything here is a static page built from data
      files anyone can review and correct.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="chronology">Dates and chronology</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Biblical chronology is contested. For now every date comes from one source, Theographic Bible Metadata, which
      follows a single traditional chronology close to Archbishop Ussher's: Abraham is born around 1996 BC and the
      Exodus falls in 1490 BC (the "early date"; many scholars prefer the 13th century BC). Dates for the divided
      kingdom's reigns also differ from widely used reconstructions such as Thiele's.
    </p>
    <p>
      We present these dates as the source gives them rather than reinterpreting them. Where scholars differ, the plan
      is to show the alternatives side by side, each with its citation. That's the next round of work on the data.
    </p>
    <p>
      People with a recorded birth and death get a <em>lifetime</em> on the timeline. Most people don't have one, so
      they appear over the years they're <em>attested</em> in: the dated events they take part in.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="eras">Eras</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    The timeline is divided into these eras. Their boundaries come from events in the same source data, so they agree
    with its chronology.
  </p>
  <dl class="eras">
    {
      periods.map((p) => (
        <>
          <dt>
            {p.name} <span class="muted">{formatSpan(p.date.earliest, p.date.latest)}</span>
          </dt>
          <dd>{p.description}</dd>
        </>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2 id="drafts">Draft records</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    The source data marks {drafts.toLocaleString()} of its {people.length.toLocaleString()} people as work in progress.
    They're shown by default, marked "draft" and in grey; the timeline and map can hide them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sources">Sources and licenses</h2>
  <ul class="sources">
    {
      sources.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{s.url ? <a href={s.url}>{s.title}</a> : s.title}</strong>
          {s.author && ` by ${s.author}`}
          {s.license && ` (${s.license})`}
          {s.notes && <div class="muted">{s.notes}</div>}
        </li>
      ))
    }
    <li>
      <strong>Easton's Bible Dictionary</strong> (1897, public domain), via Theographic, for the descriptions of people
      and places.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/">Natural Earth</a></strong> (public domain) for the map's
      coastlines, lakes, and rivers.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The data derived from Theographic is shared under the same license,
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. The code is MIT licensed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="contributing">Contributing</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Found a wrong date, a misplaced city, or a missing link between people? Corrections live as small files in the
      repository's <code>data/overrides/</code> folder, so a fix is an ordinary pull request. See the
      <a href={repo}>project on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <p><a href={url("/")}>Back to the timeline →</a></p>
  </div>
</Page>

<style>
  .eras {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .eras dt {
    font: 600 1.02rem var(--serif);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .eras dt .muted {
    font: 0.85rem var(--sans);
    margin-left: 0.35rem;
  }
  .eras dd {
    margin: 0.15rem 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .sources {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .sources li {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
    background: var(--wash);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0.05rem 0.3rem;
  }
</style>
